---
description: VibeGap project architecture and coding rules
alwaysApply: true
---

You are helping build VibeGap, a full-stack AI web app that compares social-media hype with real-world place review data.

Core idea:
VibeGap checks whether a place's online "vibe" matches reality.

Example:
Social media says: "quiet hidden gem cafe"
Google reviews say: "crowded, loud, overpriced"
VibeGap shows the mismatch.

Tech stack:
- Next.js App Router
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Server-side API routes
- Modular provider architecture
- Mock data first, real APIs later

Main product flow:
1. User searches for a place.
2. App fetches or mocks place data.
3. App fetches or mocks social hype data.
4. Truth engine compares hype vs reality.
5. App returns a VibeGap Report.

Important rules:
- Do not build everything at once.
- Build in small versions.
- Use mock data before real APIs.
- Keep external API calls server-side only.
- Never expose API keys to the browser.
- Keep shared types in lib/types.
- Keep place providers in lib/places.
- Keep social providers in lib/social.
- Keep scoring/AI logic in lib/ai.
- Avoid using any.
- Prefer clean, readable, beginner-friendly code.
- The app should look polished and portfolio-worthy.

MVP folder structure:

app/
  page.tsx
  api/
    vibecheck/
      route.ts

components/
  SearchBar.tsx
  ProcessingSteps.tsx
  VibeReport.tsx
  VisualGrid.tsx
  RealityPanel.tsx
  ScoreCard.tsx

lib/
  types/
    vibecheck.ts
  places/
    mockPlacesProvider.ts
  social/
    mockSocialProvider.ts
  ai/
    truthEngine.ts

Product style:
- Premium travel product
- Clean white space
- Modern typography
- Rounded cards
- Soft shadows
- Minimal but impressive
- Mobile-friendly

Do not add authentication, database, real TikTok scraping, OpenAI, or Google Places yet.